Negative control for the RIGHT-anchored actions column (D11).

Moves the row's controls out of the trailing cell and into the leading one, and
drops the shrink treatment that pins that column to the row's edge — the
floating, mid-table controls the operator's screenshot caught, and a data column
left holding the row's right edge instead. The header keeps its own trailing
cell, so header and row disagree exactly as they did.

Re-pointed after the canary refactor (`R6-34`/`R6-36`): the marker no longer has a
column of its own and the cell renderer is dispatched, so the trailing cell is
now the only one this mutation has to move.

Two E1 cluster cases fail alongside the two D11 anchor cases, and that is the
spec's coupling rather than a second law withdrawn: E1 locates the cluster by
reading the row's LAST cell, so moving the cell moves what E1 looks at. Nothing
about the cluster itself changes — same container, same icon-only controls, same
lock.

Apply from the repo root: `git apply <this file>`; revert with `git apply -R`.

Reds: the actions-anchor spec — every row's controls sit in the LAST cell, and
that cell shrinks to its contents.

